  "order": 216,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "rabcflp",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Drainage Patterns of India"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Himalayan rivers are perennial, antecedent, and form deep V-shaped valleys, whereas Peninsular rivers are seasonal and rain-fed.",
        "The Indus River System originates near Lake Manasarovar and flows into the Arabian Sea after traversing India and Pakistan.",
        "The Ganga River System forms the largest drainage basin in India, originating as Bhagirathi from the Gaumukh glacier.",
        "The Brahmaputra River is known as Tsangpo in Tibet, Dihang in Arunachal Pradesh, and Jamuna in Bangladesh.",
        "Major west-flowing Peninsular rivers like Narmada and Tapti flow through rift valleys and form estuaries instead of deltas."
      ],
      "id": "sh4cin1"
    },
